WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:13.500
First, the cold email. Bagas writes to an alumna he has never met. His first draft talks about himself, and asks for sometime.

2
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:21.600
The second draft shows his homework in line one. And the ask is small, specific and time-boxed.

3
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.300
The first line has one job: to earn the second line.

4
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:36.300
Second, the interruption. Bagas is thirty seconds into his update when his lead cuts in: wait, what is the bottom line?

5
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:45.300
It is not an attack. It is a request for a different altitude. He answers in one sentence, then asks: shall I go into the detail?

6
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:54.300
Third, disagreeing upward. His lead wants to spend the whole budget on one channel. Bagas thinks: that plan is wrong.

7
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.600
Said aloud, that sentence defends his ego, not the decision.

8
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:07.700
So he shares the data, names an option, and asks a real question: what am I missing?

9
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:16.200
Put the three side by side. A cold email works when the ask is small, specific and time-boxed.

10
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.900
An interruption is navigation, not attack.

11
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:25.200
A disagreement lands with data, an option and a question.

12
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.500
Each one starts from the other person, not from you.

13
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.600
The first line must earn the second.

14
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:39.100
Disagree to protect the decision, not your ego.

15
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:47.600
Now it is your turn. Draft your own version of each message before you open the debriefs below.
